From bf9f3e6f878f06db0494f9722f7fa7f0c08fac36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 00:07:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 28/32] fix(qsa): bound the shared reservation by the widest
 reachable batch

init_cuda_graph_state reserved from the calling backend's OWN geometry, and both
axes are narrower than the process-wide worst case. The window was already scaled
up by max_speculative_num_draft_tokens; the batch was not.

get_batch_sizes_to_capture filters the configured graph batch sizes by
bs * captured_req_width % alignment. The target verify runner passes
captured_req_width = W and the draft runner passes 1, so with an alignment above 1
the two resolve different max_bs -- and whichever reserved second would be asking
to GROW a buffer the first one's graphs had already captured, which the plan
refuses. That is a boot failure, on a code path whose whole point is to let the
boot succeed.

Reservations now take widest_batch: the configured graph-bs list unioned with the
admission cap. Every backend in the process therefore lands on the same row count
regardless of order, and covering the admission cap also means an eager verify
wider than every captured graph is served from shared storage instead of falling
back to a private buffer -- so the fallback path is now unreachable in practice
rather than merely harmless.

At B3c-4 (graph bs 4 = mrr 4) the number is unchanged: 67584 rows / 132 MiB.
Tests: 19 pass in the serving venv (new case asserts six different (bs, per_req) pairs
reserve one number, and that without the bound the plan refuses the second
reservation). GPU2 equivalence re-passes -- 1 allocation, 138412032 bytes, all
four tiers plus the interleave bitwise equal, 0.29 GiB peak, 5.1 s.
